se attribution of that kind.\",\n    \"Sign convention (from the evaluator source): delta_mae = baseline MAE - candidate MAE and delta_score = candidate utility - baseline utility, so positive favours the candidate. State this once. The best baseline by utility is the random subset mean (best-baseline and random-mean deltas are identical).\",\n    \"Bootstrap intervals (95% percentile, 300 replicates; resampling benchmarks, then seeds, then target models) include zero for the best-baseline gap on both MAE and utility; phrase as 'point-estimate improvement not statistically resolved'. No CI is available for the anchor-points and IRT-style deltas, so do not call those significant either; but the anchor and IRT-style deltas are larger in point estimate. No per-regime baseline numbers exist: do not state that the method helps more for random or strong targets than baselines do.\",\n    \"Baseline MAEs shown in figures are derived (candidate MAE + reported delta): random mean 0.0339, anchor points 0.0428, IRT-style 0.0692; label them 'derived from reported deltas'. Baseline Spearman values are not available; do not plot them.\",\n    \"Variant comparison is a cumulative-change comparison on 2 search seeds; there is no single-factor ablation (ablation_studies is empty). Do not attribute the c01->c02 gain to a specific component (logistic model vs Fisher design vs stacking) and do not describe c03 as hurting; it is statistically indistinguishable. The top-two gap (0.0006) is below within-variant seed spread, so present selection of c02 as the frozen choice, not as a demonstrated best.\",\n    \"Planned analyses in the idea files (k sweep over 20-200, 20+ seeds, MMLU-Pro/BBH emphasis, latent-rank and shrinkage ablations, temporal split) were NOT run; the evaluator uses only k=100. Do not claim them; list the k sweep and ablations as untested in the limitations.\",\n    \"The drop from search utility 0.9452 to final 0.9314 occurs across disjoint benchmarks and different seed