{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0118WcRU15gUkdysdMBqCpdE","type":"tool_result","content":"60:\\paragraph{Subset selection for benchmark prediction.} Anchor points \\citep{Vivek2023AnchorPB} cluster items by the response vectors of source models and score a new model by a cluster-size-weighted average over representative items. tinyBenchmarks \\citep{Polo2024tinyBenchmarksEL} combines such anchors with item response theory (IRT) to predict full scores, and metabench \\citep{Kipnis2024metabenchA} uses IRT-based information to select items and fits a predictor on top of them. Efficient benchmarking \\citep{Perlitz2023EfficientB} studies how much a large benchmark can be reduced while preserving model rankings, and PromptEval \\citep{Polo2024EfficientME} applies related ideas to the variation introduced by prompt formats. ONEBench \\citep{Ghosh2024ONEBenchTT} studies how to aggregate sparse sample-level measurements into model scores and rankings. Our baselines follow the clustering and IRT families; the baseline implementations are the evaluator's own re-implementations rather than the original code.\n64:\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-by-item matrix. The final estimator follows the model-assisted idea of correcting a model-based prediction with an observed-sample residual, in the spirit of prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI}. Item selection by greedy log-determinant maximization is D-optimal experimental design \\citep{Kiefer1960TheEO}, and the combination of estimators with non-negative weights fitted on held-out predictions is stacked generalization \\citep{Wolpert1992StackedG}. Benchmark scores themselves vary with seeds and prompts \\citep{Madaan2024QuantifyingVI}, which motivates reporting uncertainty for the gain over simple baselines.\n169:Table~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three configurations of the family on the development benchmarks (two seeds each; all three were scored, none failed). The utility is 0.9381 for the linear model-assisted estimator, 0.9452 for the selected stack and 0.9445 for the stack with bagged posterior means and tail calibration. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.combined_score]]\n223:Each episode is identified by a seed, a benchmark and a regime. For random targets the evaluator draws $\\max(5,\\operatorname{round}(0.2\\,M))$ models as targets, where $M$ is the number of models with results on the benchmark, and uses the rest as sources. For strong targets, the pool is the set of models with full accuracy at or above the 0.7 quantile; sources are models strictly below it, and the targets are drawn from the pool (at most the same number). Episodes require at least 40 sources and 5 targets. The source matrix is transferred in at most two chunks of bit-packed rows. The selector must return exactly $k{=}100$ distinct valid indices, and the predictor must return one finite prediction per target. Aggregation proceeds from seeds to benchmarks (equal weight) to regimes (0.5/0.5); failed or missing episodes would contribute worst-case values, but none occurred in either evaluation (22 and 32 episodes completed, 0 failed). The development benchmarks are IFEval, ARC-Challenge, MuSR, GSM8K and BBH; the held-out benchmarks are GPQA, MedQA, LegalBench, MMLU and MMLU-Pro. Candidate processes ran on CPU with a bounded wall-clock budget per episode; the number of observed candidate calls was 88 in the development evaluation and 128 in the final evaluation.\n247:Utility & 0.9381 & 0.9452 & 0.9445 \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c01.combined_score]]\n223:Each episode is identified by a seed, a benchmark and a regime. For random targets the evaluator draws $\\max(5,\\operatorname{round}(0.2\\,M))$ models as targets, where $M$ is the number of models with results on the benchmark, and use